      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Preheat the oven to 180C. To make the milk cream, whisk together the plain flour and sugar in a medium saucepan. Gradually whisk in the cold milk until smooth with no lumps."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place the saucepan over medium heat and cook, stirring constantly with a whisk, until the cream thickens to a custard-like consistency, about 8-10 minutes. Remove from the heat and stir in the rose water and honey. Allow to cool slightly."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Brush a rectangular baking tin (approximately 30x20cm) with melted butter. Lay 4-5 sheets of filo pastry in the tin, brushing each with melted butter and allowing the edges to overhang."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Scatter half the toasted almonds over the pastry base. Pour the cooled milk cream evenly over the almonds."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Fold the overhanging pastry up over the filling. Layer 4-5 more sheets of buttered filo on top, tucking the edges under neatly. Brush the top generously with the remaining melted butter."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bake for 25-30 minutes until the pastry is deep golden and crisp. Allow to cool for 10 minutes in the tin."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Scatter the remaining almonds over the top. Dust generously with icing sugar and a good shake of ground cinnamon. Serve warm or at room temperature."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The milk cream has lumps"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Lumps form if the flour is not fully dissolved before heating, or if the cream is not stirred constantly. If you spot lumps early, use a whisk vigorously and the heat will usually smooth them out. If the cream is already thick and lumpy, pass it through a sieve before using."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The pastry is soggy underneath"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make sure the milk cream is properly cooled before pouring it onto the pastry. Hot cream will steam and soften the filo before it has a chance to crisp in the oven. The scattered almond layer also helps protect the base."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The top is browning unevenly"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Ensure each layer of filo is brushed all the way to the edges with melted butter. Dry patches brown more slowly than buttered ones. Rotate the tin halfway through baking if your oven has hot spots."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Pastilla au Lait is a modern classic of Moroccan patisserie, served in cafes and sweet shops across the country. Unlike the savoury meat bastilla from which it takes its name, this is a purely sweet dish built around a silky milk cream scented with "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "orange-blossom"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "orange blossom water"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " or rose water. It is a street food as much as a formal dessert, bought by the slice and eaten standing up. The contrast between the shattering crisp pastry and the cool set cream inside is what makes it so distinctive. It is best eaten the day it is made."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
